# CDK context cache. Regenerated by `cdk synth`/`deploy` and keyed by account
# (AZ lists, and previously a Route 53 hosted-zone lookup), so it is neither
# source nor portable to another account. Untracked 2026-08-11 so account-
# specific lookups never reach a published snapshot.
cdk.context.json

# ProtoShield security scanner.
#
# protoshield-output/ and .protoshield/scans/ hold scan runs (reports, raw
# scanner output, logs). They are regenerated on every scan and are not source.
#
# .protoshield/config.yaml IS tracked, deliberately. It was ignored until
# 2026-08-15, and that cost real work: the license-headers plugin was still on
# its default `license_text: Apache 2.0` while this repo's LICENSE is MIT-0, so
# a scan reported all 202 source files as non-compliant. The prescribed fix --
# add Apache-2.0 headers -- would have made every file declare a license the
# repo does not grant. Because the config was machine-local, that trap was
# per-clone: fixing it here would not have protected anyone else's scan.
# Scanner configuration that encodes a correctness decision belongs in the repo.
#
# It stays out of the PUBLIC snapshot via .publish-exclude (ProtoShield is an
# internal tool), so this only shares settings with the private repo.
.protoshield/scans/
protoshield-output/

# Syncthing conflict artifacts -- never commit
*.sync-conflict-*


# Downloaded security-scanner exports -- never committed.
#
# These are findings dumps pulled out of a scanner's UI for triage. They are
# inputs to a review, not source, and the repo root is published, so an
# accidental `git add .` would ship one.
#
# Until 2026-09-13 the only thing keeping them out was remembering to delete
# each file by hand -- which worked twice and is not a control. The patterns are
# deliberately generic rather than naming today's scanner, because the next one
# will export under a different name: `*ScanExport*` covers the CSV dumps,
# `*-scan.csv*` the per-tool ones (including a `.csv.numbers` re-save), and
# `*.numbers` any spreadsheet saved from one.
#
# Triage conclusions belong in the repo's tracked scanner-findings triage notes.
# The export itself is disposable; the decision about it is not.
*ScanExport*
*-scan.csv
*-scan.csv.*
*.numbers
